// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    The Dilemma (Morton's Fork / Sadistic Choice)
// @category Drama / Choice
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Sadistic_Choice
// @domain   Mind
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): a character is presented two alternatives, both untenable — choose A and
// something dear is lost, choose B and something else dear is lost, with no third way out. Morton's
// Fork is the version where both forks gore the same; the Sadistic Choice is the version played for
// drama, a cruel hand forcing the pick between two things the agent cannot bear to lose. FRONTIER:
// the philosophy module's MoralDilemma RECOGNIZES the standing antinomy (two ends in mutual
// conflict); The Dilemma adds the FORCING and the COST — an event imposes the pick, and each horn
// is a mortal life, so that the act of choosing is itself the ordeal.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/modules/philosophy.trl"   // the MoralDilemma concept; imply Moral_Dilemma -> [Conflicted, Binding, Generative]

// Lateral thematic links (optional, machine-followable — for discovery / embeddings).
assoc the_dilemma -> [
  trolley_problem  : "is the lived form of",
  utilitarianism   : "is the price exacted by",
  deontology       : "is the price exacted by",
  heroic_sacrifice : "is what the unchosen horn demands"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// The Dilemma is a Moral_Dilemma with no escape: each of its two horns is a mortal stake, and the
// cruelty is that there is no third option. Morton's Fork (the two forks gore the same) and the
// Sadistic Choice (a forcer twists the knife) are its two faces.

imply Dilemma        -> [Moral_Dilemma, Inescapable, Narrative]
imply MortonsFork    -> [Dilemma, Equivalent]
imply SadisticChoice -> [Dilemma, Coerced, Cruel]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Recognition: an agent is committed to two distinct ends that stand in mutual conflict (the
// MoralDilemma core, `><`), each end is a mortal life ([%Mortal]) the agent will lose if the other
// is chosen, AND an event has forced the pick. The mortal stakes on BOTH horns are what raise a mere
// conflict to The Dilemma — losing either is unbearable, and choosing is the ordeal. The prompt is
// the choice itself: each branch keeps one horn and severs the other (its bearer will die), and
// either way the agent is left [#Guilty] over the horn they could not take.
rule The_Dilemma {
  when:
    char $c
    char $horn_a [%Mortal]
    char $horn_b [%Mortal]
    $c [~Committed_To(target=$horn_a)]
    $c [~Committed_To(target=$horn_b)]
    $horn_a != $horn_b
    $horn_a >< $horn_b
    evt $force [&Confronted(other=$c)]
  then:
    $c [+Dilemma(between=$horn_a)]
    $c [#Conflicted]
    prompt(
      target   = $c
      question = "Two you cannot lose — and you must lose one. Which?"
      choices  = {
        "Keep the first; the second is lost." : {
          $c -- $horn_a : "Keeps"
          $c !-- $horn_b : "Committed"
          $horn_b [=Dying]
          $c [#Guilty]
        }
        "Keep the second; the first is lost." : {
          $c -- $horn_b : "Keeps"
          $c !-- $horn_a : "Committed"
          $horn_a [=Dying]
          $c [#Guilty]
        }
      }
    )
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: Sophie's Choice — the selection at Auschwitz (novel, 1979) ===
// On the ramp at Auschwitz, an SS doctor — idle, drunk on his own power — tells Sophie she may keep
// one of her two children; the other goes to the gas. If she chooses neither, he will send both.
// There is no third way: the cruelty is engineered. She screams that she cannot choose, and the
// choosing is the wound she carries the rest of her life. Both horns are a child's life; keeping one
// is paid for in the other.

// SOPHIE ZAWISTOWSKA
char sophie [+Protagonist]

// DR. FRITZ JEMAND VON NIEMAND (the selecting SS physician)
char doctor [+Antagonist] [+Cruel]

// JAN — Sophie's son
char jan [%Mortal]
// EVA — Sophie's daughter
char eva [%Mortal]

// EXT. THE SELECTION RAMP, AUSCHWITZ-BIRKENAU - NIGHT
set the_ramp [+Exterior] [+Night]

scene the_selection {
  beat 1 {
    sophie @ the_ramp
    doctor @ the_ramp
    jan @ the_ramp
    eva @ the_ramp
    // a mother committed to both children; keeping either is the death of the other
    sophie [~Committed_To(target=jan)]
    sophie [~Committed_To(target=eva)]
    jan >< eva : "Either Child Is The Other's Death"
  }
  beat 2 {
    // the forcer imposes the pick — and seals off the third option (refuse, and both die)
    evt the_demand [&Confronted(other=sophie)]
        dialog(inject=[speaker=doctor], inline="""
               speaker: "{speaker}"
               cue: "smiling, a riding crop tapping his boot, the line shuffling past"
               tone: idle, sadistic
               text: "You may keep one of your children. The other one will go. You may keep one."
               """)
    // The_Dilemma fires: sophie [+Dilemma]; [#Conflicted]; the prompt is the choice itself
    sophie [+Dilemma(between=jan)]
    sophie [#Conflicted]
  }
  beat 3 {
    // she takes the only move left to her — she keeps the daughter, and pays in the son
    evt she_chooses [&Confronted(other=sophie)]
        dialog(inject=[speaker=sophie], inline="""
               speaker: "{speaker}"
               cue: "the words torn out of her, already destroying her"
               tone: shattered
               text: "Take my little girl! Take Eva!"
               """)
    sophie -- eva : "Keeps"
    sophie !-- jan : "Committed"
    jan [=Dying]
    // the choosing is the ordeal — the guilt is the cost no branch escapes
    !sophie [#Guilty]!
  }
}
